---
/**
 * LeadFormTracker.astro
 *
 * Instruments a Formspree-backed lead-capture form so a successful submission
 * fires a GA4 event we can use to measure conversion. Without this, MDG has
 * 5 form endpoints that POST to Formspree and zero visibility into which
 * forms are actually being used, what stages operators are at, or which
 * services they're requesting.
 *
 * Usage in a page that contains a form:
 *   <LeadFormTracker formSelector=".lead-capture-form" formName="download_checklist" />
 *
 * Props:
 *   formSelector  REQUIRED. CSS selector for the <form> element. Each MDG form
 *                 has a unique class (e.g. .lead-capture-form, .referral-form,
 *                 .newsletter-form, .founder-bible-form).
 *   formName      REQUIRED. A snake_case event label. Used in GA4 as
 *                 `lead_capture.form_name`. Keep it stable — GA4 reports will
 *                 pivot on this string.
 *   trackFields   OPTIONAL. Array of field names whose values should be
 *                 included in the GA4 event. Defaults to
 *                 ['stage', 'interest', 'service']. All optional; missing
 *                 fields are simply omitted from the event payload.
 *
 * What it does:
 *   - Listens for `submit` events on the matched form(s).
 *   - Reads each tracked field from the form before submission.
 *   - Fires `gtag('event', 'lead_capture', { form_name, stage, interest,
 *     service, page_path })` if gtag is available.
 *   - Does NOT preventDefault or otherwise interfere with the Formspree POST.
 *     The form submits normally; the GA4 event fires alongside it.
 *   - Wraps everything in try/catch so a tracking failure can never break
 *     the actual lead capture.
 *
 * Why client-side (not server-side) tracking:
 *   - Formspree POSTs are external. There is no server-side hook on MDG
 *     (static site) to observe the submission.
 *   - gtag is loaded on every page by Layout.astro.
 *   - The submit event fires before the form is POSTed, so we capture intent
 *     even if Formspree later rejects the submission. (Treat the GA4 event
 *     as "user clicked submit on a lead form", not "Formspree accepted".)
 *
 * Verification: build the site, open a form page, fill it out, submit. In
 * DevTools console, look for `gtag('event', 'lead_capture', ...)`. Or, in
 * GA4 DebugView (or GA4 Realtime), filter on event_name = "lead_capture".
 *
 * Sprint 76 observability: closes gap #4 from the 2026-06-07 MDG tracking
 * audit. Prior state: 5 Formspree forms, 0 GA4 conversion events.
 *
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Note on 2026-07-05 split: TWO different "lead captured" events
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * After the lead-capture endpoint wiring (apps/maine-cannabis/src/pages/
 * api/lead-capture.ts + vercel.json route), the /download/first-timer-
 * field-guide landing page intercepts its form submit, POSTs JSON to
 * /api/lead-capture, and fires the GA4-recommended `generate_lead` event
 * on a successful 200 response — NOT `lead_capture`. The other 4
 * Formspree-backed forms continue to use this component and fire
 * `lead_capture` on submit-intent (Formspree doesn't have a webhook hook
 * that would let us fire `generate_lead` on server-side confirmation).
 *
 * In GA4, this means:
 *   - lead_capture (intentionally counted on submit-intent): all 5 lead
 *     forms. Slight over-count if Formspree later rejects, but matches
 *     the existing dashboard convention dating from Sprint 76.
 *   - generate_lead (recommended, only fired by first-timer-field-guide):
 *     semantically more accurate (server-confirmed) but only 1 of 5 forms
 *     uses it today.
 *
 * The intent is to migrate the other 4 Formspree forms to the same
 * /api/lead-capture pattern over time, at which point all 5 lead forms
 * would fire `generate_lead`. Until then, treat `lead_capture` as the
 * historical/back-compat event and `generate_lead` as the new standard.
 *
 * If you're instrumenting a NEW form: prefer the /api/lead-capture
 * flow with `generate_lead`. If Formspree remains the right tool for
 * the form (e.g. operator-facing forms where dashboard access matters
 * more than conversion accuracy), use this LeadFormTracker component
 * with `lead_capture`.
 */

interface Props {
  formSelector: string;
  formName: string;
  trackFields?: string[];
}

const { formSelector, formName, trackFields = ['stage', 'interest', 'service'] } = Astro.props;
---

<script is:inline define:vars={{ formSelector, formName, trackFields }}>
  (function () {
    try {
      // Defer until DOM is ready so we can find the form(s).
      function init() {
        var forms = document.querySelectorAll(formSelector);
        if (!forms || forms.length === 0) {
          // Silent: page may not contain the form (e.g. partial renders).
          return;
        }
        forms.forEach(function (form) {
          if (form.__mdgLeadTracked) return; // idempotent across re-runs
          form.__mdgLeadTracked = true;
          form.addEventListener('submit', function () {
            try {
              if (typeof window.gtag !== 'function') return;
              var payload = {
                form_name: formName,
                page_path: window.location.pathname,
              };
              trackFields.forEach(function (name) {
                try {
                  var el = form.elements.namedItem(name);
                  if (el && 'value' in el && el.value) {
                    payload[name] = el.value;
                  }
                } catch (_) {
                  // Skip this field; continue with the rest.
                }
              });
              window.gtag('event', 'lead_capture', payload);
            } catch (_) {
              // Tracking must never break the actual form submit.
            }
          });
        });
      }
      if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', init);
      } else {
        init();
      }
    } catch (_) {
      // Component-level init failure is silent by design.
    }
  })();
</script>
